Subject: [PATCH 19/36] B70-QSA-Q03: QSA side-cache state-contract tests and an
 engine lifecycle driver

tests/models/qwen4_exp/test_qsa_state_contracts.py drives one layer's ring
and compressed-key cache through engine-shaped step sequences and checks the
state after every step against a ground truth built from committed tokens
only (draft keys differ from committed ones):
- spec rollback: every residue mod 4 x acceptance 0..7, plain decode between
  verifies, 60-step random runs (ring wrap, page crossings), the 832 block;
- prefix hit / CPU reload with and without the EAGLE trailing-block drop;
  characterizations of an unaligned hit and a missing compressed reload;
- repeated execution as C1 capture does it (idempotent for 8-row verify,
  padding rows, null block untouched), real graph capture/replay on GPU, and
  the long-prefill non-idempotence that keeps target graphs verify-only;
- the 2,048 + 3 boundary (selection = whole prefix through 2,051 tokens;
  sparse == dense attention on GPU) for plain and speculative rows;
- selector identity under ties: native_det == torch exactly (CPU with an
  adversarial native stub; GPU with the real op, repeats, graph replay).
The ref backend runs on CPU; triton/graph cases need XPU or CUDA.

tools/b70_qsa/qsa_engine_lifecycle.py compares cold, GPU-hit, CPU-reload,
unaligned-junction, cancel+reuse and interleaved paths of one prompt on a
running engine: greedy tokens (near-tie aware) and the dumped compressed rows.
